# Vulnerability details

## Bug Description

The `LiquidityPool` uses an algorithm that converts prices to shares through a linear relationship. For instance, if there are 10,000 assets and 100 shares, then holding 100 assets should yield 1 share.
However, a situation arises when investor sends only 99 assets. In such cases, the conversion rounds down to zero, resulting in zero shares.

It's important to note that no one would intentionally make such a financial loss. Nevertheless, a potential threat arises when an attacker can frontrun a `LiquidityPool.requestDeposit`.

## Proof-of-Concept

A test scenario illustrating changes in the assets-to-shares ratio:

Upon witnessing an investor initiate a `LiquidityPool .requestDeposit` with a 99 currency amount, an attacker frontruns the transaction with a higher gas price, submitting a 10,000 currency amount request. In this case, the investor who was frontrun will receive zero TrancheTokens.

