# Vulnerability details

## Impact

It can lead to a significant loss of tokens for users of the `LiquidityPool` contract an attacker can repeatedly withdraw tokens, potentially draining the contract's token balance.

## Proof of Concept

The contract allows users to deposit and withdraw assets, and it interacts with the ERC20 token using `transfer` and `transferFrom`. These functions can trigger external contract calls. If users or other contracts call external functions in response to token transfers, it could lead to reentrancy attacks.

The potential area of concern where reentrancy attacks could occur is in functions that interact with external contracts, particularly when using `transfer` and `transferFrom` functions from an ERC20 token. Reentrancy vulnerabilities typically involve external contract calls made within the context of these functions.

- In the **[transfer](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/512e7a71ebd9ae76384f837204216f26380c9f91/src/LiquidityPool.sol#L301-L305)** function.
