# Vulnerability details

## Impact

An attacker, who is also the contract owner, can manipulate the `latestPrice` variable within the LiquidityPool contract to exploit the asset to share conversion functions. The attacker artificially inflated the `latestPrice` to undervalue shares and assets during conversions, potentially causing financial losses for users.

## Proof of Concept

In functions like [`convertToShares`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/512e7a71ebd9ae76384f837204216f26380c9f91/src/LiquidityPool.sol#L115-L120) and [`convertToAssets`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/512e7a71ebd9ae76384f837204216f26380c9f91/src/LiquidityPool.sol#L122-L127), the contract calculates the number of shares or assets based on the provided input. These calculations are dependent on the `latestPrice`, which can be manipulated if not properly secured. It's crucial to implement checks to verify that the `latestPrice` is reasonable and within expected bounds.

Example:
```solidity
function convertToShares(uint256 assets) public view returns (uint256 shares) {
    require(latestPrice > 0, "Price not available");
    // Implement a check to ensure that the calculated shares are reasonable
    require(shares <= MAX_CALCULATED_SHARES, "Invalid conversion");
    shares = investmentManager.convertToShares(assets, address(this));
}
```
Share conversion calculations being dependent on the potentially manipulated `latestPrice` occurs in the following lines of code within the `LiquidityPool` contract:

```solidity
