    function isAllowedAsPoolCurrency(uint64 poolId, address currencyAddress) public view returns (bool) {
        uint128 currency = currencyAddressToId[currencyAddress];
        require(currency != 0, "PoolManager/unknown-currency"); // Currency index on the Centrifuge side should start at 1
        require(pools[poolId].allowedCurrencies[currencyAddress], "PoolManager/pool-currency-not-allowed");
        return true;
    }
}
``` 
the problem is the currencyAddressToId mapping is being used to look up the currency ID associated with currencyAddress and if the currencyAddress is not found in the mapping, it returns 0, indicating an "unknown currency", so it's doesn't check if the provided currencyAddress is a valid Ethereum ERC20 token address.
let's suppose an attacker provides an arbitrary address which is not a valid ERC20 token contract as currencyAddress. in that case, the currencyAddressToId mapping will return 0 because the address is not in the mapping. As a result, the function will incorrectly assume that the provided address is an "unknown currency" and return true. this means the attacker can trick the contract into thinking any address is an allowed pool currency, potentially leading to unintended behavior or security issues.
