# Vulnerability details

## Impact

If an ERC20 token like WETH is added as an asset to a liquidity pool then an attacker can cancel another user's deposit or deposit approved assets.

 [`requestDepositWithPermit`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/main/src/LiquidityPool.sol#L223) calls `permit` on assets via the `ERC20PermitLike` interface. For tokens that implement the [ERC2612](https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-2612) standard this will revert if the signature (v,r,s) is not ecrecover'ed to the `owner`'s address.

However if any ERC20 token is added that does not implement `permit` the fallback function will be called and it may not revert. In the case of WETH it will call the fallback() then deposit(0) but not revert and continue executing the [`requestDepositWithPermit`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/main/src/LiquidityPool.sol#L223) function. See the code below; 

```
function requestDepositWithPermit(uint256 assets, address owner, uint256 deadline, uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s)
    public
{
    ERC20PermitLike(asset).permit(owner, address(investmentManager), assets, deadline, v, r, s);
    investmentManager.requestDeposit(assets, owner);
    emit DepositRequested(owner, assets);
}
```

For ERC20 tokens like WETH an attacker can cancel any deposits requested by calling `requestDepositWithPermit` with 0 for assets, the valid depositors address and a valid deadline. V, r and s can be any value. Assets set to 0 is checked by the if statement in [`requestDeposit`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/main/src/InvestmentManager.sol#L127) in [`InvestmentManager.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-09-centrifuge/blob/main/src/InvestmentManager.sol) and the order is cancelled without the valid depositor/owner's approval.


