# Vulnerability details

## Impact
`Auth.deny` will directly remove permissions from the user, even if the address is the `msg.sender` himself.
Once all `wards` have been removed, then no new `wards` could be added because there are no more `auth` users that could call `Auth.rely`. This could result in a permanent denial of service, as functions related to authentication cannot be called, and recovery is impossible.



```
    /// @dev Give permissions to the user
    function rely(address user) external auth {
    //@audit rely new user need `auth`, if `wards` is empty, no one has the permission to rely.
        wards[user] = 1;
        emit Rely(user);
    }

    /// @dev Remove permissions from the user
    function deny(address user) external auth {
    //audit deny could remove `msg.sender` himself, this is dangerous.
        wards[user] = 0;
        emit Deny(user);
    }

    /// @dev Check if the msg.sender has permissions
    modifier auth() {
        require(wards[msg.sender] == 1, "Auth/not-authorized");
        _;
    }
```




